# Development and production workflow

## One build root, tracked in Git

Python files under `icon_set/model/icons/` are the drawing source. The build
output is `published/`, and Git tracks it. There is no separate development
build, no publication staging folder and no copy step: `build`, `dev`,
`publish` and `release` all read and write `published/`.

```sh
python3 -m icon_set build --icon icon_set/model/icons/solo/example.py --no-png --no-report
python3 -m icon_set dev --open            # serves published/ with the local database
python3 -m icon_set publish --dry-run     # list new, changed and missing icon outputs
python3 -m icon_set publish               # changed-only build, validate, compact, release.json
python3 -m icon_set doctor
git add published icon_set
git commit -m "Publish updated icon catalog"
git push origin icon-lib
```

`publish` builds the changed originals in place, refuses embedded manual
artwork or runtime files, compacts every JSON catalog so Git diffs stay small,
and writes `release.json`. Before writing, it reports newly registered icons,
drawings whose SVG content changed, and missing or stale generated SVG, metadata
or preview files. It regenerates those outputs across every family and always
refreshes the gallery links. Validation failures remain visible in the Failed
build view; their artwork is excluded from the passing family manifests.

On production, keep the **existing production database and all sibling state
folders** outside the checkout. Pull the same branch, then serve the assets:

```sh
git pull --ff-only origin icon-lib
python3 -m icon_set production \
  --database /srv/pictographic/state/feedback.sqlite3 \
  --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

`production` defaults to `published/`. It does not build, generate icons,
modify source files, or import the local progression snapshot. Reviews,
approvals, comments, uploads and selected manual edits stay in its own
persistent state. Restart the server when Python server code changes; asset-only
pulls are picked up by the running server.

## Ownership

| Area | Location | Tracked | What belongs here |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Authoring | `icon_set/model/icons/` | yes | Python originals created or revised by agents |
| Editorial source | `icon_set/metadata/` | yes | Curated names, tags and descriptions |
| Supporting datasets | `icon_set/data/` | yes | Combination pairs, container trials, content areas, profile links |
| Experiments and evidence | `icon_set/work/`, `icon_set/assets/` | yes | Experiment sources, reference SVGs, experiment snapshots |
| Build output | `published/` | yes | SVG exports, manifests, gallery, failed builds, reports, PNG previews |
| Validation evidence | `published/qa/` | no | Per-icon QA overlays and metrics, regenerated by every build |
| Runtime state | `icon_set/state/` | no | Review database and backups, uploads, stroke edits, agent jobs, keys |
| Retired output | `icon_set/.local/`, `icon_set/dist/`, `icon_set/assets/previews-png/` | no | Old build folders kept on disk only; nothing uses them |
| Production state | A separate external directory | no | Persistent production database and manual artwork |

The shared paths live in `icon_set/scripts/workspace.py`: `build_dist()`,
`preview_dir()`, `state_root()` and the `DEFAULT_*` constants. Use those
definitions when adding tools. Model contracts still name family subfolders
(`solo48/`, `sub32/`, ...), independently of the build root.

The editable container/side pair database has an explicit import and export
workflow under `python3 -m icon_set combinations`. See
[Combination database](combination-library.md). It lives in runtime state;
builds never seed it or overwrite its edits.

## Daily work

Disapproved icons are fixed through the shared production work queue so two
machines never repair the same icon: `python3 icon_set/scripts/work_queue.py next`
sets one to the review status Claimed on production and prints its brief,
`done` reports the fix and returns the revision to Ready, `cannot-fix` and
`abandon` release it. A claim older than six hours goes back to Disapproved. A local
`dev` server forwards every `/api/work*` request to its `--sync-source`, so the
review grid on localhost shows production's claims. See
[Work claims](work-claims.md).

The development Primitives page also reads its catalog, classifications, briefs
and counts from `--sync-source` through the local server. Primitive edits are
forwarded to the same production authority. It does not fall back to local
progress when production is unavailable; local icon builds do not count as
production completion until deployed.

Read a generation queue page without a running HTTP server using
`python3 icon_set/scripts/generation_queue.py 40 --offline`. This reads the
workspace's published primitive catalog and local review database in read-only
mode, using the same queue logic and classification history as the gallery.
For another local gallery, supply `--dist PATH --database PATH` with `--offline`;
both paths must match that gallery. Missing data is an error, not an empty queue.
Omit `--offline` to keep using the HTTP endpoint.

```sh
python3 -m icon_set doctor                       # paths, tracked state, uncommitted output
python3 -m icon_set typeface-sizes               # export the centerline typeface
python3 -m icon_set build --no-png               # full catalog (rarely needed)
python3 -m icon_set build --icon icon_set/model/icons/solo/example.py --no-png --no-report
python3 -m icon_set dev --open                   # local app on published/ with icon_set/state/
```

For a single authored icon, use the compact verification helper:

```sh
python3 -m icon_set finish-icon icon_set/model/icons/solo/example.py
```

It runs one targeted build, reports actionable validation findings, checks the
export against its manifest hash, and renders a single light/dark preview sheet
at native and enlarged size. Inspect that sheet for fidelity. Full diagnostics
are saved beside the previews in a temporary directory (`--out DIR` overrides).
Use `--dist DIR` for isolated tests. Numeric success is not visual approval.

Targeted builds update only selected metadata, gallery entries, provenance,
related primitive/combination links and failure summaries. They preserve
unrelated exports, reference assets, UI files, experiments, sidecars and catalog
records, including records with no source in the current checkout. Full builds
and publish still refresh the complete gallery. No Git-based cleanup is needed.

A targeted build keeps previously built icons. Use `--all` when a full
revalidation is required. Builds do not create source metadata;
`python3 -m icon_set.scripts.sync_metadata` is an explicit library-wide
editorial seeding operation, not part of creating one icon.

Agent candidates get private workspaces and build folders, with the same CLI
and authoring instructions. Accepting a candidate affects the local build only.
No build imports saved manual artwork unless `--artwork-dir` explicitly requests
a manual export. Such an export is not accepted as a production baseline.

Build, publish and release-export commands share an output lock on
`published/`. If the folder is busy, the second operation fails without
writing a partial result. Retry after the first operation completes.

## Release and rollback

An exported release is an immutable snapshot of `published/` outside the
checkout, for deployments that need atomic switching rather than a Git pull:

```sh
python3 -m icon_set release /srv/pictographic/releases/release-001
python3 -m icon_set production \
  --dist /srv/pictographic/releases/release-001 \
  --database /srv/pictographic/state/feedback.sqlite3 \
  --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

Export refuses to replace an existing release, rejects baked-in manual choices,
and records a catalog fingerprint and icon counts in `release.json`. A build
refuses to write into an exported release; only `published/` carries a
`release.json` and accepts builds. To roll back, restart with the previous
release and the **same state directory**. No database is copied from the
developer machine as part of release export.

Production enables manual uploads, editing, source selection, and reviews. It
has no agent manager, and rejects generation, source deletion, catalog
regeneration, and replacement of its database through the sync endpoint.
Development-only controls are hidden based on `/api/runtime`; the API enforces
the boundary too.

